<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philaret Drozdov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vasily Mikhaylovich Drozdov, Metropolitan Philaret, Filaret</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philaret, born Vasily Mikhaylovich Drozdov, was Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and the most influential figure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> from 1821 to 1867. Born in Kolomna to a clerical family, he taught at the Troitse-Sergiyeva Lavra and the academy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> Lavra, became Archbishop of Tver in 1819, moved to Moscow in 1821 and became metropolitan in 1826. A leading preacher and author of many theological works, he worked toward a Russian translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and is said to have prepared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>'s proclamation freeing the serfs; he also had Old Believer churches sealed and their clergy imprisoned. He was canonised in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philaret-drozdov.jpg" alt="Philaret Drozdov" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Hau (1817-1895) / Владимир Гау · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filaret_(Drozdov)_by_Gau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vasily Drozdov was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> on 26 December 1782, the son of a member of the clergy. He studied at the seminary of Kolomna, where teaching was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and then at the Troitse-Sergiyeva Lavra, which made him a professor as soon as he finished; he taught Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and poetry. He became preacher of the lavra in 1806, and in 1808 he received the monastic tonsure and the name Philaret, after Saint Philaret the Merciful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1809 he became professor of theology at the ecclesiastical academy of the Alexander Nevsky Lavra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>, archimandrite in 1811 and director in 1812. The events of 1812 made a strong impression on him: he explained the Russian success by moral causes in a lecture to the Society of friends of the Russian word, and in 1813 delivered a speech on the death of Kutuzov. He was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Reval and episcopal vicar of St. Petersburg, and after Tver he was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> in 1820 before his move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p>His daring utterances brought him imperial disfavour from 1845 until the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>. He was the spiritual father of the missionary hieromonk Macarius (Glukharyov), who was canonised in 2000 as the Apostle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> he went as far as the misappropriation of churches and the sealing of the altars at the Rogozhskoye Cemetery, the administrative and spiritual centre of the Belokrinitskoe Soglasie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Before his time only a Church Slavonic Bible, not readily understood by ordinary people, was available. For a period the Minister of Education opposed translations into the vernacular, and from 1825 to 1856 Philaret turned instead to teaching and clerical duties.</p><p>His many volumes of theological and historical writing are known together as the Filaretica. They include the Colloquy between a Believer and a Skeptic on the True Doctrine of the Greco-Russian Church (1815), a Compend of Sacred History and a Commentary on Genesis (both 1816), the Christian Catechism (1823) and the New Collection of Sermons (1830–36). He wrote spiritual poems from an early age, and his poetical correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> is well known. He died on 1 December 1867, and his feast day is 19 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philaret Drozdov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Philaret Drozdov</b>, a cleric’s son who became a monk in 1808, headed the see of Moscow from 1821 to 1867 and was the church’s leading figure.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.08,38.78&amp;year=1837" aria-label="Kolomna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Philaret Drozdov"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5428 5392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5428 5392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.08,38.78&amp;year=1837">What was here in 1837? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
